so if we want to think scientifically  about cooking we have to start by  thinking a little bit about what food is  made out of because after all it is the  constituents of food which will be the  major player in this adventure that  we're about to launch ourselves on let's  just start with the very basic fact  which is that all of us and all of our  food is made of molecules now there are  simple molecules there's the molecules  that's in the air around us there's for  example carbon dioxide there's oxygen  there's water  these are simple molecules they contain  a of course themselves are components of  atoms so carbon dioxide is composed of  carbon and oxygen water is composed of  hydrogen and oxygen and so on food  molecules are more complex than atoms  and there are also more complex than  simple molecules the molecules are  cooking namely fats proteins and  carbohydrates tend to be much larger  molecules which themselves also contain  atoms such as carbon nitrogen oxygen and  so on and one of the unusual things  about cooking is is that the basic  molecules are through the basic fats the  basic sugars that we will talk about  tend to form very large networks you  know or sort of agglomerated structures  which are what you know make up the  structure of food and also actually for  that matter of us so let's just start  with fats so fats are very common so  examples of common fats and that you  might eat with include olive oil coconut  oil cocoa butter shortening and that has  you know sort of two features that I  that I would like to highlight right now  so the first is is that fats can either  be in liquid or in solid form at room  temperature and indeed there are fats  that are liquids there are fats that are  solids at room temperature so the second  feature of fats is that fats don't  dissolve in water that's why when you  take try to make salad dressing and you  mix oil and water for example olive oil  and water you will notice that the olive  oil does not actually mix itself in the  water and even if you take your bottle  of olive olive oil and water and shake  it up rapidly they will if you let it  sit for a while on the countertop  separate em into the oil being on top  and the water being beneath it and that  is because oil and water  mix with each other they said  differently what oil doesn't dissolve in  in water now this is sort of one of the  defining features of oil and it is very  different than the two other major  components of food so now let's talk a  little bit about carbohydrates so  carbohydrates consists basically if  sugars you know sucrose you know the  sweet taste that you tend to eat if  you're trying to eat something sweet and  also chains of sugars which are starches  you know we're sort of longer chain  sugars and these are the sort of two  main types of carbohydrates that exist  in your food  so either sugars or sugar chains now  sugars actually have their own  interesting properties so one property  of sugar which at least I find  interesting is that sugar is really a  very hard substance that is if you try  to scratch it a solid sugar piece of  sugar it's it's very difficult to do so  for example imagine taking rock candy  rock candy is really a solid it doesn't  squish at all and compare that to either  your skin you know or to jello or to  something like that which is tends to be  squishy sugar is very hard and this  hardness as we will discover in this  class is a property of the molecules and  that make it up so the other thing which  is interesting about sugar is that sugar  dissolves very well in water the  solubility limit of sugar is two pounds  per pound and that's really sort of  remarkable a remarkable property of  sugars so as I said actually the other  main type of carbohydrate are starches  and starches are what we call polymers  that is chains of sugar molecules so  what you do is you take simple sugar  molecules like glucose or sucrose and  you put them together in long chains and  those chains are what makes up starches  so examples include for example amylose  which is a polymer of glucose monomers  so you take a bunch of glucose monomers  and stick them together end by end to  make a long chain and that makes up  amylose or there is what people call  amylopectin which is the same thing  except instead of being a linear chain  like polymer it's actually branched so  there are branches of polymer that are  coming off of it in different ways in  this course many of our chefs will  introduce you to new molecules like for  example Agra Ferran adrià is a great fan  of agar agar agar  there's pectin there's guar gum there's  methyl cellulose there's jell n there's  xantham gum there's a huge host of  molecules which all are actually  carbohydrates so whereas flour is made  by from wheat by milling and grinding  wheat you know guar gum actually comes  from plants agar comes from seaweeds  Gellin and xanthan gum come from  microbes and there's a whole host of  different molecules that are made by  organisms on our planet that and we can  use and we do use as part of our foods  and we will see in the course of this  class that that remarkable things can be  made when you enlarge the set of  molecules that's at your disposal so now  I want to turn to the third major  component of food which is proteins so  proteins of course occur in many of the  things that you eat so for example if  you look at a piece of steak the red  color and steak comes from hemoglobin at  least in part which is a protein  constituent of blood so milk actually  has a protein in it that is called  Casson  albumin is a protein component of eggs  which is the thing that is responsible  really for the remarkable properties of  eggs when you heat and you cook them  this is something that we will talk  about extensively in this class and  proteins when considered as a molecule  have their own interesting property  which is distinct from that of either  fats or carbohydrates and that is the  following so proteins dissolve in water  and that's the same as carbohydrates but  what proteins do that is different is  that when you heat up water or you heat  up the protein by a relatively small  amount you know say 50 degrees Celsius  even is often enough you can get the  protein to change conformation the  protein is an active molecule it doesn't  keep the same shape as you cook it and  indeed it is the changes in shape and  configuration of proteins that arguably  are really at the heart of most of  cooking when you apply heat or other  means as we will again see and  throughout this class so what is a  protein so a protein is also a polymer  it's a polymer in the same way that the  starch molecules that I described before  polymers except in this case  instead of the monomers all being  identical for example being you know a  glucose or a simple sugar molecule  proteins are polymers of amino acids and  it turns out that there are 20 different  types of amino acids that compose the  proteins that are part of life as we  know it and these 20 amino acids these  20 monomers themselves have different  physical properties some of them  dissolved in water some of them don't  dissolve in water some of them have  electric charge some of them don't have  electric charge and as we go through  this class we will start to see how the  different components of proteins give  rise to the transformations that occur  when we cook them what happens when you  cook a protein when you heat a protein  is that the long strand tends to unfold  into a long extensible polymer so one  can unfold and much of cooking it turns  out is related to the transformation in  which a protein in a given food is  actually extended into a long polymer  and when it does that then it turns out  that the polymers that is the different  proteins that are in the food tend to  stick to each other and they sort of  coagulate and you form a gelatinous mass  and that process the changing of a  protein from a compact shape to a big  gelatinous mass of many proteins  interacting is you know at the heart of  cooking an egg it's at the heart of  making jello it's at the heart of many  of the food transformations that we will  see in this class and these can occur as  we will see both by applying heat or by  applying changes in pH by actually using  acid Monbiot lemon juice or vinegar or  by changing the salt concentration and  so these are the various knobs that are  at play with proteins when you cook
 

